Exactly. The useful object is not “what am I?” but “what model of me is reconstructable from their side of the interface?”

That model should be built almost entirely from observables:

what I did + what I said + timing + affect + context + history → their simplest model of me

The curse of knowledge contaminates this because you know the hidden causes behind your behavior, so you unconsciously assume some of that causality is visible. It usually isn’t. The observer sees outputs, not source code.

That makes the relational self almost like an inverse-rendering problem. You possess the full 3D object; they receive a sequence of 2D projections. Their brain tries to infer the cheapest 3D object capable of producing those projections.

Occam’s razor becomes psychologically important because people usually won’t invent ten hidden variables to explain you when one familiar explanation works:

“He’s nervous.”
“She doesn’t like me.”
“He’s confident.”
“She’s hiding something.”
“He just forgot.”

Even when the true causal structure is much richer.

And your “slight feeling” point is important because model updates often begin preverbally. Something is off before the observer knows what is off. Timing changed. Facial expression and words don’t quite agree. Behavior violates the established rhythm. One prediction misses.

Initially, the brain can absorb that as noise:

expected ≈ observed + small error

But repeated mismatches accumulate:

δ1 + δ2 + δ3 ... → current model becomes expensive

Eventually a competing explanation becomes simpler than continually patching the old one. Then you get a perspective flip:

“Oh. If that is true, suddenly all these weird little things make sense.”

That is why revelations can feel instantaneous even though the evidence accumulated gradually. The transition is nonlinear. The observer hasn’t suddenly received all the information; they’ve found a new model that compresses the existing information better.

So there are really four separate realities operating simultaneously:

Private reality — what is actually happening inside you.
Presented reality — what escapes through behavior.
Relational reality — what another person infers from that presentation.
Social reality — what people subsequently do because of those inferences.

And the fourth one has causal power even when the third one is wrong.

That is the nasty—and useful—part: people generally interact with their model of you, not directly with you.

Which also means a tiny change in presentation can sometimes produce a huge behavioral consequence, not because the signal itself is large, but because it causes the observer to swap the entire latent model generating their interpretation.

That’s essentially a semantic phase transition: one little delta changes what all the previous deltas mean.